--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0077 (fill Section 2 of the journal article) revealed a
recurring authoring need: for every paragraph that cites external
work, the author wants a deeper **due-diligence note** that sits
alongside the manuscript. The note is what an author reads before
defending a paragraph under peer review.

Since this need is not specific to 0077 — it applies to every future
manuscript, every Related Work section, every paragraph with a
non-trivial citation list — the note-production workflow is captured
as a reusable **skill**, not as one-off scripts inside 0077.

The skill is **user-level** (`~/.claude/skills/related-work-note/SKILL.md`)
because the need recurs across every manuscript the operator writes,
not just this repo. User-level placement follows the operator's
harness rule that generic-purpose tools live outside project
vendoring. The skill is therefore not committed to this repo;
ticket 0077 consumes it via the user-level install.

## Requirements

### Skill metadata

- Location: `~/.claude/skills/related-work-note/SKILL.md` (user-level, not vendored into the repo)
- Frontmatter: `name`, `description`, `user-invocable: true`,
  `argument-hint: [paragraph-topic]` (or a small config reference).
- Invocable via the Skill tool.

### What the skill produces

A single markdown note file per invocation, with this invariant
structure:

```
---
title: {paragraph topic — imperative noun phrase}
author: Claude prompted by Ha-Duong Minh
date: {YYYY-MM-DD}
paper: {path to parent manuscript}
section: {section number and title, e.g., "§2 Related Work, paragraph N"}
---

## Relevance

Why this note exists for this paragraph of this paper. What claim
the paragraph makes and what a referee could legitimately probe.
One paragraph.

## History of science context

The lineage of the sub-field the paragraph sits in — the problem's
origin, the main paradigm shifts, the current state of the art.
200–400 words. Enough for the author to position cited works
against the field, not the other way around.

## Cited works — detailed

For each citation the paragraph uses:
- Full reference (author, year, venue).
- What the work actually did (method, data, main result — 2–4
  sentences).
- Why this paragraph's argument needs it (the specific claim it
  supports).
- Known limitations or critiques the author should be aware of.

## Related but not cited — justified

Works a reader might expect to see, each with a one- or two-sentence
justification for non-inclusion (superseded, off-topic, redundant,
weaker evidence, proprietary/closed, not yet peer-reviewed, …).

## Methods

How this note was written (LLM side):
- Searches run (query, tool, date).
- Databases consulted (Google Scholar, Semantic Scholar, arXiv,
  operator's Zotero library, …).
- Freshness cutoff (deep research includes results through {date}).
- Preprint policy: arXiv preprints allowed when no peer-reviewed
  equivalent exists; flagged as such in the entry. Preferred when
  covering 2024–2026 frontier.
- LLM-assist disclosure — drafted by Claude; cited facts must be
  verified against the primary source before publication.

## Author verification checklist

What the author must still do before the paragraph is defensible
(separate from the LLM-side disclosure above):
- [ ] Opened and read each cited primary source (not just its
      abstract).
- [ ] Confirmed the claim-to-citation mapping for every sentence.
- [ ] Checked that preprints flagged in Methods are still the
      best available citation (peer-reviewed version may now
      exist).
- [ ] Agreed with the "Related but not cited" justifications.

## Bibliography

Alphabetical by first author, BibTeX-compatible. Every entry
carries DOI (when available) and a stable URL.
```

### What the skill's procedure must do

1. Accept the paragraph's topic/claim and the target manuscript path
   as inputs. Derive an output filename and title.
2. Deep-research the topic: run web/scholar searches for anchors in
   the sub-field, fresh 2024–2026 results, and any known superseded
   work. Document every search in the Methods section (query,
   tool, date, result count).
3. Cross-check the operator's Zotero library for existing matching
   references (see `memory/reference_zotero.md`) before minting new
   BibTeX keys. If a ref exists in `report/refs.bib`, use that key.
4. Draft each cited-work entry with method + main result + specific
   claim + limitation. Drafts without all four fields are rejected.
5. Justify every related-but-not-cited entry in one or two
   sentences. No unjustified stubs.
6. Populate the Methods section verbatim (queries, cutoff,
   LLM-assist disclosure).
7. Write the Bibliography with DOI/URL on every entry. Entries
   lacking DOI and lacking a stable URL are rejected.
8. Emit the note file to a caller-specified path (the paper's
   `notes/` or `due-diligence/` sibling directory).

### Non-requirements (out of scope for this ticket)

- Running the skill on ticket 0077's three paragraphs. That belongs
  in 0077; this ticket only proves the skill works on a pilot.
- A GUI or CLI wrapper. Skill invocation via the built-in Skill
  tool is the only interface.
- Integration with BibTeX build or citation-insertion tooling.
  The skill produces a markdown note and bibliography block; human
  or a downstream skill moves refs into `report/refs.bib`.

## Actions

1. Read existing skill patterns under `.claude/skills/` (e.g.,
   `ticket-new/SKILL.md`) to match house style.
2. Draft `.claude/skills/related-work-note/SKILL.md`.
3. **Narrow pilot.** Run the skill on a small, self-contained
   paragraph topic (not one of 0077's paragraphs — pick something
   narrower, e.g., a single sub-claim such as "table-understanding
   benchmarks for LLMs"). Place the pilot note in
   `notes/skill-pilot/narrow/`.
4. **Broad rehearsal.** Run the skill a second time on a
   paragraph-3-scale topic that spans multiple sub-literatures
   (e.g., "AI-assisted maintenance of energy infrastructure
   databases" — wide enough to stress the skill but not identical
   to 0077's paragraph 3). Place the rehearsal note in
   `notes/skill-pilot/broad/`. This check catches skills that pass
   a narrow pilot but choke on real-scope paragraphs.
5. Verify both notes pass the structural checklist (see Test).
   Iterate skill instructions until they do.
6. Commit skill + both pilot notes. Open a PR. Operator reviews
   and approves.
7. On approval, ticket 0077 is unblocked and can consume the skill.
   Each 0077 invocation re-runs the skill freshly — notes are not
   a reusable cache; Methods freshness reflects invocation date.

## Test (first thing to build)

Both pilot notes (narrow, from step 3; broad, from step 4) must
pass this checklist:

- [ ] YAML frontmatter has title, author, date, paper, section.
- [ ] All seven sections present: Relevance, History of science
      context, Cited works — detailed, Related but not cited —
      justified, Methods, Author verification checklist,
      Bibliography.
- [ ] Each cited work has all four fields (reference,
      what-it-did, why-we-cite, limitations).
- [ ] Each non-cited entry has a one- or two-sentence
      justification.
- [ ] Methods section names at least one fresh (2024+) search
      result and states a preprint policy.
- [ ] Author verification checklist is present and distinct from
      Methods.
- [ ] Every bibliography entry has DOI or stable URL.

The broad rehearsal (step 4) is a harder bar than the narrow
pilot: the skill must produce a coherent note even when the topic
spans several sub-literatures. If the broad rehearsal fails, 0077
is not unblocked.

## Exit criteria

- `.claude/skills/related-work-note/SKILL.md` committed and
  invocable via Skill tool.
- Two pilot notes (narrow + broad) committed under
  `notes/skill-pilot/`, both passing the structural checklist.
- Operator has reviewed and approved skill + both pilots.
- Ticket 0077 explicitly unblocked (update its `Blocked-by`).

## Relationship to other tickets

- **0077** — consumer of this skill. Blocked by this ticket.
- **0075** — adjacent authoring doc (`docs/quality-grounding.md`);
